---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import LeadStrand from '../components/LeadStrand.astro';
import Shot from '../components/Shot.astro';
import Needs from '../components/Needs.astro';
import Draft from '../components/Draft.astro';

const B = '/work/alucify';
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="An interface for supervising work you did not watch happen"
  metaTitle="Alucify — designing for AI agents working on a codebase | Tyler Young"
  description="A code-intelligence tool whose primary metrics are blast radius, agent hours and token cost — designed for people accountable for work an agent did."
  descriptionDraft
  eyebrow="01 · AI-Native Product"
  meta={[
    ['Product', 'Code intelligence — “your code, flows, and intents, unified”'],
    ['Contribution', '[NEEDS: what Tyler owned here, in his words]'],
    ['Surface', 'First run through tenured use · 20 documented states'],
    ['Status', 'Active venture · shown with the founder’s agreement'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="answer">
    <Draft>
      When an agent does the work, the human job changes. You are no longer watching
      it happen — you are arriving afterwards and deciding whether to trust it. This
      is an interface built for that moment, and its primary metrics say so.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <Shot
    src={`${B}/dashboard.png`}
    alt="Alucify dashboard for a project called RBAC v1.1.1, showing artifact status of one integrated, seven critical issues and twelve major issues; coverage analysis at 89 percent overall with three partially covered and one not covered; and impact analysis showing 87 percent blast radius, 240 agent hours and a 5M token cost."
    state="Tenured user dashboard"
    caption="Blast radius, agent hours and token cost sit at the same level as coverage. Customer name redacted in the rail."
    wide
  />

  <h2>The metrics are the argument</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Blast radius. Agent hours. Token cost. Those are not vanity numbers — they are
      the three questions someone actually has when an agent has been let loose on a
      codebase: how far did this reach, how much work was done, and what did it
      cost to do. Putting them on the landing surface at the same weight as coverage
      is a claim about what the product is for.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Cost as a first-class metric is the part most tools get wrong. It is treated
      as billing, tucked into a settings page, and therefore never influences a
      decision. Here it sits next to the outcome it paid for.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>Coverage is a judgement, not a score</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      A single percentage tells you nothing you can act on. The coverage view breaks
      the number back into the decisions underneath it — each one named, categorised
      and marked covered, partial or not covered — so the question stops being “is
      89 per cent good” and becomes “which four things are not covered, and do I
      care about them.”
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <Shot
    src={`${B}/coverage.png`}
    alt="Coverage analysis view showing 89 percent overall across 27 total decisions, with 23 fully covered, 3 partially covered and 1 not covered, above a table of named AI decisions each tagged none, partial or covered."
    state="Coverage analysis"
    caption="Every row is a specific decision — a gateway type resolution spec, a cache hydration model — not an abstract category."
    wide
  />

  <h2>Severity earns the interruption</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      The impact view is where the product is allowed to be loud. A scoped change
      boundary marked critical, two of five, with its own summary and a breakdown
      across blast radius, regression risk, data integrity and backward
      compatibility. Everything else on the surface is quiet so that this can be
      heard.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <Shot
    src={`${B}/impact.png`}
    alt="Impact analysis detail panel for a scoped change boundary marked critical, two of five, with a written summary and severity indicators for blast radius at 30 percent, 56 agent hours, 3M token cost, regression, data integrity and backward compatibility."
    state="Impact analysis — concept detail"
    caption="Severity is carried by a badge, a colour and a written summary together, not by colour alone."
    wide
  />

  <h2>The states nobody designs</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Twenty screens, and a good number of them are the ones usually left to
      engineering to improvise: the integration mid-flight, the audit that has gone
      stale and needs re-running, the dashboard before anything exists in it. A
      developer tool is used mostly in these states, not in the populated one.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <div class="pair">
    <Shot
      src={`${B}/reaudit.png`}
      alt="Artifact re-audit view with a full-width orange banner stating the artifact was integrated on a given date, a deprecation notice reading this audit has been deprecated, please re-audit, and a table of six issues tagged critical, major and minor, each paired with a written resolution."
      state="Deprecated audit"
      caption="A stale result is stated plainly and paired with the action that fixes it."
    />
    <Shot
      src={`${B}/in-progress.png`}
      alt="Artifact integration in progress, showing the dashboard skeleton with empty metric slots and a yellow status banner explaining that context is being created and a completion notification will follow by email."
      state="Integration in progress"
      caption="The empty state explains what is happening and what will end the wait."
    />
  </div>

  <h2>What I owned</h2>
  <p>
    <Needs>
      Tyler — the honest scope line. Same standard applied to word web: concept,
      direction, design, execution — which of these were yours, in your words.
    </Needs>
  </p>

  <div class="aside">
    <p>
      Shown with the founder's agreement. The customer name has been redacted from
      the navigation rail in every screen; the product and its data model are
      otherwise as designed.
    </p>
  </div>

  <LeadStrand
    slot="after"
    items={[
      {
        slug: 'how-i-managed-ux-complexity',
        note: 'A tool that reports blast radius and token cost is measuring complexity so a person can act on it. That is the complexity budget, pointed at an agent rather than a design team.',
      },
      {
        slug: 'how-i-infused-design-roles-with-product-thinking',
        note: 'Choosing which three numbers belong on the landing surface is product work. It decides what the tool is for before a single screen is laid out.',
      },
    ]}
  />
</DocumentLayout>

<style>
  .answer {
    font-size: 24px !important;
    line-height: 1.52 !important;
    margin-bottom: 36px !important;
  }

  .pair {
    display: grid;
    gap: 20px;
    margin-bottom: 34px;
  }

  .pair :global(.shot) {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  @media (min-width: 1000px) {
    .pair {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }
</style>
